{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1403018-what-is-scope-and-population-of-a-typical-changeling-chronicle","id":1403018,"title":"What is scope and population of a typical Changeling Chronicle?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-07-15T17:32:55-07:00","id":1403018,"title":"What is scope and population of a typical Changeling Chronicle?","content":"What is the typical chronicle size that you play in (whether it is as a PC, or as an ST running a chronicle)? By that I mean, what is the general scope geographically, the number of Fae creatures in the background (including all kinds - Kithain, Gallain, Thallain, Inanimae, Chimerical creatures, etc.), supporting creatures, and the number of actual &quot;named&quot; NPCs that you interact with (or could potentially interact with)?<br /> <br /> I know what I would answer in some of the other games.<ul><li>For Vampire I would say that the game takes place in one metropolitan area, and that it includes anywhere from 30-70+ vampires and probably around 100 ghouls (assumed), but that in practice the PCs could probably interact with all vampires (but much fewer ghouls) depending on their actions. It is very rare for PCs to travel outside this area, but it does happen in some chronicles.</li> <li>For Werewolf, I would say the game mainly takes place in large geographic area (the equivalent of one large US state or multiple states, or similar area in Europe) and contain 4-6 local septs plus the Penumbra. There are probably around 50-100 Garou and potentially thousands of kinfolk, but most likely the PCs interact with only 20-30 Garou in the base sept, and if they do travel to other septs they'd only interact with another dozen or so Garou at most. While the game might include travel to even more septs further out, or Umbral Realms, they are generally reserved for ST plots. There might be just as many named/main enemy characters such as Fomori, BSDs, PENTEX personnel, etc.</li> <li>For Wraith, I would say the game mainly takes place in a major city that holds a Necropolis and Citadel and perhaps associated domains outside the main city which the Necropolis has administrative jurisdiction over. The local wraith population is in the hundreds or even thousands, but the actual number of Wraith NPCs interacted with on a named level is probably a few dozen. Travel to the Sea of Shadows, Stygia, and elsewhere is fairly routine, but most NPCs met are merely background/flavor text as opposed to real characters.</li> </ul>I'm sure there are many who quibble about this in their own home chronicles, but I'm fairly certain I've described most chronicles.<br /> <br /> But I don't know how a Changeling game &quot;should&quot; look. How many different freeholds are part of the setting? How many different barons, counts, dukes, kings do PCs keep track of? What's the general population of Kthain and other Fae? How about chimerical creatures (as opposed to background things like chimerical animals that may be there as background but aren't really interacted with)? How many Enchanted humans or Kinain? What's &quot;normal&quot;?<br /> <br /> I don't know if there is one. Perhaps people will be all over the place.<br /> <br /> Besides stating what is the case in the chronicles you actually play in, it is also OK to state what you think it should be if it is different (larger, smaller, or any other major demographic divergence). And if you have your own idea of what the &quot;standard&quot; size is.<br /> <br /> Thank you. Looking forward to reading any responses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-15T17:44:16-07:00","id":1403020,"title":"","content":"It's been a really long time since I've done a Changeling game, but most of them tended to be along the same scale as Vampire or Wraith, usually a single city or small town. Usually starting with a focus on a single freehold/neighborhood and building upward and outward as the PCs grew in experience and importance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T11:14:22-07:00","id":1403125,"title":"","content":"I've been slowly expanding a setting in a major European metropolitan area for my game. This is how I've been developing it. As you can see I'm very generous with the number of freeholds. I figure most of these would be largely in the Near Dreaming, with buildings and landscapes connected to entrances in the Autumn World:<br /> <br /> - one Freehold for every noble house, functioning as the leader's seat and house assembly hall.<br /> I consider that some houses might have just a dozen or less individuals in the city, including nobles, titled commoners and servants. This would be the case with House Balor in my game, the one I have fleshed out more so far. I guess larger houses like Gwydion might reach as much as 50 members, around 20 titled and 30 servants. <br /> <br /> - one Freehold for each court, where house leaders can convene.<br /> <br /> - at least one Freehold serving as a meeting ground for both Seelie and Unseelie courts.<br /> <br /> - one Freehold where most political dealings between commoners and nobles take place.<br /> I guess this would be the equivalent of the Parliament of Dreams if the setting was in Concordia.<br /> <br /> - a Freehold where commoners gather to offer services, like a Goblin Market. This could be one large and/or several smaller divided by Kith and trade specialty.<br /> <br /> - several and varied commoner Freeholds, that would be smaller and more anchored in the Autumn World.<br /> <br /> - a secret Freehold for the Shadow Court.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-18T17:56:11-07:00","id":1403448,"title":"","content":"I think it depends mainly on how much you want to stress Banality's threat to the fae.<br /> If you want to downplay it, you can have large Courts and several freeholds all over the city, with a healthy amount of changelings.<br /> However, if you want a Heavy-Banality game, a tiny Court of roughly 100 changelings per city would bring home how few survivors are still out there<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10198-luisarmander","name":"Luisarmander","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-18T19:11:53-07:00","id":1403462,"title":"","content":"With the usual disclaimer of the Golden Rule and what follows is only my opinion aside, here's my take:<br /> <br /> The average of standard Changeling the Dreaming game takes place in a large enough geographic area to be considered a barony, whether this is a city or a neighborhood or a stretch of small towns or suburbs.<br /> <br /> The barony is ruled over by a baron who maintains a feudal relationship with a group of lords who rule households under the baron's command while also claiming the fealty of commoner-held households, if there are any. There is a probably a small handful of households to allow for different noble houses of the baron's court to be present and perhaps a strange outsider or commoner freehold.<br /> <br /> The area contains anywhere between a dozen to several dozen changelings with kinain equaling a fraction of the number of changelings and the number of enchanted at any one time being ~1.5-5x the number of changelings. These enchanted alongside serving chimera likely make up the lowest rungs of changeling society.<br /> <br /> The area is filled with a rich chimerical environment that mirrors reality with the Near Dreaming and likely a few notable landmarks in the nearby Far Dreaming with trods to connect them. Chimera populate the Dreaming in a similar way to spirits in the Umbra although with arguably even greater interaction with changelings then spirits have with changing breeds. <br /> <br /> Most changelings are likely to be associated with either a motley or a household, if not both. The tapestry of Nobility, Loyal Commoners, Commoners, and Outlaws provide a spectrum of changeling society and behavior with kinain, enchanted, and chimera joining every culture. <br /> <br /> The player's motley have or will most likely interact with the other changelings, kinain, and chimera of their social group. They are likely to be aware of most other changelings as well, particularly significant ones and notably powerful chimera.<br /> <br /> The area also most likely contains elements of Nightmare enclaves hidden where such things can flourish and potentially holding Black Courts, Shadow Courts, Thallain, Nightmare Chimera and foolish or risky Unseelie.<br /> <br /> There are also most likely barren places that reek of Banality which are known to host Autumn People and that changelings may fear hosts Dauntain as well or could threaten to create one.<br /> <br /> Depending on the history of your play region there may also be non-European kiths present such as the Nunehi, Hsien, Jinn, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T05:46:53-07:00","id":1403513,"title":"","content":"Gryphon gives a good account of things.<br /> <br /> I want to try introducing the concept of Chimera\u2019s oathing fealty to the courts and houses to bolster the Dreaming population of a local duchy. The notion being Chimera are somewhat more real when connected to Changelings, they start to become more self aware as mentioned in a few books. So when Nobles start accepting chimera citizens your court swells and becomes a rather sizeable population of individuals to interact with.<br /> <br /> I also like the idea of random disappeared humans becoming Chimera, so they have human sentience and agency but for some reason they become chimerical life forms and their family forgets about them because of the mists. They don\u2019t have arts and only a handful of redes at first but they make excellent citizens.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T15:13:18-07:00","id":1403580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1403018-what-is-scope-and-population-of-a-typical-changeling-chronicle?p=1403462#post1403462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The average of standard Changeling the Dreaming game takes place in a large enough geographic area to be considered a barony, whether this is a city or a neighborhood or a stretch of small towns or suburbs.<br /> <br /> The barony is ruled over by a baron who maintains a feudal relationship with a group of lords who rule households under the baron's command while also claiming the fealty of commoner-held households, if there are any. There is a probably a small handful of households to allow for different noble houses of the baron's court to be present and perhaps a strange outsider or commoner freehold.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for a fairly comprehensive answer. I have a few follow up questions though.<br /> <br /> Do you mean baron/barony or count/county (or even duke/duchy)? You mention lords owing fealty to the baron, but baron is the lowest of noble titles. Or do you mean families of Sidhe (or other noble families) that simply are not titled?<br /> <br /> Also, what do you mean by households? Do you mean motleys? Freeholds? Something else?<br /> <br /> Finally, about how many freeholds are in this area?<br /> <br /> = = =<br /> The game seems to be inconsistent in the number of Freeholds out there. It's part of PC Backgrounds, so it is possible for multiple PCs to start the game with it. But in other sections, it mentions Barons typically have only one Freehold. And it is unclear to me how distributed they are among the general population. How many freeholds &quot;should&quot; there be for the general Changeling population - ration of Changelings per freehold (perhaps by # of levels of freehold instead of by # of freeholds)?<br /> <br /> Also, what are people's ratio of &quot;nobles&quot; to &quot;commoners&quot;? In the real world, this was extremely rare. But Changeling seems much more top heavy than real life. So maybe 10% or more of the population is noble.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T21:30:52-07:00","id":1403616,"title":"","content":"Barons are indeed the lowest of the Fae Noble Lords, Not every Baron even has a Barony. Below Baronies are Fiefs which can be held by a Baron without a Barony or a Knight.<br /> <br /> Back in the day Sidhe used to be about 5 percent of the Kithain, and just a guess but maybe Commoner nobles number about the same. So that makes about 10 percent.<br /> <br /> Oh and remember in France they said they have a 4 nobles per commoner ratio, which seems bonkers but I guess they are supposed to be the Capital of Europes Fae Nobles but still seems odd.<br /> <br /> Since revised the most solid guess I heard about the Changeling numbers is at least 100k Kithain, considering the way they describe their courts and how populated and filled out positions are, but they might have double that number of Fae in Between Chrysalis/reincarnations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T23:31:11-07:00","id":1403629,"title":"","content":"Glad ya like it and Eldagusto makes interesting points, but lemme answer to the best of my ability!<br /> <br /> \u2022Barony &amp; Titles of Nobility<br /> Like Eldagusto mentioned, Changeling Society follows its own hierarchy of titles which in the Kingdom of Concordia goes something like this: High King (ya boi David) -&gt; King/Queen -&gt; Duke/Duchess -&gt; Count/Countess -&gt; Baron/Baroness -&gt; Lord/Lady &amp; Knight/Dame -&gt; Squire &amp; Esquire (with Viscount and other titles added for spice at your liege lords pleasure)<br /> <br /> The reason I say that a typical setting is probably a Barony, although a small scale game could readily take place in a single lord's household, is that a Barony offers enough variety and number of nobility and noble households to create a diverse environment and enough changelings to create a Byzantine web of noble intrigue for the Sidhe to amuse themselves with.<br /> <br /> \u2022Household<br /> When I say household I refer basically to any gathering of changelings in a similar way that one might invoke a caern or chantry. These are probably freeholds although not necessarily, but they are places which changelings can safely gather and engage in their forms of society.<br /> <br /> There are numerous sorts of households. There are Noble Households presided over by a lord or lady of the nobility and who claims the fealty of the changelings dwelling within their house. These noble households are also probably pledged to a certain noble house and court, that of the house's lord, and by extension the residents are also indirectly pledged. These are the noble establishments <br /> of the olden days. Their are Commoner Households or Freeholds which are often led by respected graybeards but not always. These resemble the freeholds of the These resemble the freeholds of the Interregnum with commoners banding together for security and can run the gamut of Loyalist to Outlaw. There are sometimes even Kith Households where kiths can gather to engage in their own society, but these require significant enough numbers and influence to make such an investment reasonable. These are Troll Lodges, Satyr Tragos, Redcap Peel Towers, and Nocker Towns.<br /> <br /> \u2022Population Ratios<br /> Much like most gamelines, imo the canon numbers rarely make much sense unless you want to play a certain sort of way and the inconsistencies of setting description don't help.<br /> <br /> What follows is my personal preference, but adjust as needed as YMMV.<br /> <br /> I generally have it that the majority of significant freeholds in an area are maintained by the nobility. The Baron has the largest and most powerful, followed by their peerage of lords, and following them are the commoner households with few exceptions.<br /> <br /> This is not due to any lack of ability on the Commoner's part or weakness in the art of stewardship. It is a direct result of the Sidhe Supremacist polices brought about by the Accordance War and remains a grounds of contention to this day.<br /> <br /> However, there are most likely a handful of Commoner Freeholds in the barony. Most are boltholes kept alive on embers of balefire that support a motley or small kith gathering at best. The significant commoner freeholds may rival the size and power of a noble household but are likely to be far more populated and supported by the common majority.<br /> <br /> Changeling populations are distributed into three broad and often co-pollinating groups. Loyalists are those who dwell in noble households with lordly protection in exchange for the obligation of obedience and service to said lord. General Commoners are the largest group and they tend to dwell in pocket households, commoner freeholds, and - naturally - the autumn world. Outlaws are lucky if they can claim a true household but may have secret pockets of glamour they maintain on the outskirts of a barony or in places the nobility can rarely tread.<br /> <br /> My interpretation of the setting is that the Nobility are probably outnumbered by the Commoners at - ~10:1 ratio. However thanks to certain arts, treasures, organization, alliances, and perhaps even the Dreaming; they are able to generally maintain control over western changeling society. The average noble has more individual influence then the average commoner.<br /> <br /> However the commoners do outnumber the nobility and while they may not have the fancy gifts that the nobility hoarded in Arcadia, they can join cohesion and cunning to their numbers. Commoners are better suited to survival in the Autumn World after centuries of practice. So the nobility maintains it rule through a careful balance of compromise, authority, and (when necessary) extreme power. They do (generally) recognize a need to heed the needs and opinions of the commoners though. Most Commoner Courts are dubbed legitimate for ruling on Commoner crimes and commoners can be represented in the Parliament of Dreams.<br /> <br /> The system is not perfect, as the support for Outlaw groups and the Shadow Court show, but it creates a stable system... even if it seems that it is ever on the brink of an age of revolution...<br /> <br /> So, all of that said, I generally populate a barony with anywhere between 3-8 freeholds on average; with perhaps a few small Freehold 1 pockets scattered where they can survive and a population ratio that tilts to the Commoners even as the Nobility claims the majority of significant freeholds.<br /> <br /> (Note: this post does not factor in the 1-3 additional freeholds/glamour wells that may be occupied by thallain, nunnehi, menehune, Inanimae, etc.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}